PLAN IS TO MOSTLY EDIT THIS REPO ONLINE 

The important thing is that this app does not use npm, 
Vite, React, or a normal JavaScript build system. 

It is deliberately a plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript PWA. 
Your repository's own developer guide confirms that tools/build.py 
is the build step.

When you add a new language pack to the git repo online
Suppose you added:
languages/de/pack.json


You need to clone the repo onto your computer

git clone https://hamzabawumia:<PAT>@github.com/hamzabawumia/My-First-Language-Book-V2.git
RECALL THAT THIS IS A PUBLIC REPO SO YOU CANNOT SHARE YOUR PAT HERE.

THEN 
cd My-First-Language-Book-V2

If you already have the repo locally then cd into the directory and use:

git pull origin main 

Then from the root of the repository, run:
python3 tools/build.py

You should see the script validate the language packs and finish with:
OK

The script automatically regenerates these three important files:
languages/index.json
data/embedded.js
sw.js

Do not edit those three manually
.
Then commit ALL of these changes
For example:
git add .
git commit -m "Add new language pack"
git push origin main

If you also added new pictures, make sure they're committed too:
images/011.jpg
images/012.jpg
...

The complete workflow I recommend
# 1. Get the latest code
git pull origin main

# 2. Run the app's build/validation
python3 tools/build.py

# 3. Test locally
python3 -m http.server 8000

Then open:
http://localhost:8000


Then:
# 4. Commit everything
git add .
git commit -m "Add new language pack"

# 5. Push to GitHub
git push origin main

One particularly important thing for your app
Your app is a PWA with a service worker. That means you can successfully push the new language to GitHub and still see the old version on your phone/tablet because the old files are cached.
Your build script updates the cache information in:
sw.js

That's why running:
python3 tools/build.py

before committing is important.
